{"author":{"id":"417295a7cc0952a9a38b67be3a894daed91f9069f7407e038d12a4f9b9a8451e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792401868,"nonce":"fa2zs5n91XxzIQ7E8wPfpPi5rqA=","title":"Native FFI: libraries, pointers, out-params, structs, callbacks, worker lane","message":"Needed by the Git built-in extension (RayDE's built-in extensions plan, libgit2). Today `native \"lib\" { function f(a:Int):Int; }` parses and compiles to `Op::BindNative` with the spec `lib\\0name\\0signature\\0flags`, and the VM fails with \"native libraries are not available yet\". The VM has no bytes type. `HostObj` holds a `Box\u003cdyn Any\u003e` that is dropped when the collector sweeps it.\n\n\nTypes in declarations:\n- `Int8`..`Int64`, `UInt8`..`UInt64`, `SizeT`, `Bool`, `Float`, `Double`. `Int` stays the VM's 64-bit integer, converted at the call.\n- `CString`: argument is a UTF-8 NUL-terminated copy; result is copied into a script String at once (the C side owns the memory); a null result is nil.\n- `Ptr`: opaque address, a host object; address 0 is nil.\n- `Out\u003cT\u003e`: a pointer to a slot the call writes (`git_repository_open(out, path)`); the script reads `out.value` afterwards.\n- `Bytes`: a new VM kind for raw data (blobs, `git_buf`); passed as pointer and length, and read back by copy.\n- `struct`: declared layout with C alignment, passed by pointer; fields read and written by name; `alloc` gives zeroed memory; arrays of structs by index (`git_strarray`, `git_status_entry`, `git_diff_delta`).\n- `callback`: a declared C function-pointer type with a `Ptr` user-data argument (`git_status_cb`, `git_diff_line_cb`, `git_credential_acquire_cb`, `git_transfer_progress_cb`), made from a script closure, passed as an argument or stored in a struct field (`git_remote_callbacks`).\n- Not in v1: structs by value, variadic functions, unions.\n\nHandles:\n- A binding can declare a free function and an owner: `git_repository_free`; a commit or tree is freed before its repository, so a handle keeps its owner alive (the collector traces it) and frees itself on drop, on the VM thread, never inside a native call.\n- `close()` frees at once and makes the handle nil-like; a use after `close` throws.\n\nCalling:\n- Prepare each signature once at bind time (a call interface per function); no per-call allocation for scalar signatures. Candidate engine: `libloading` for loading and `libffi` for calls and closures (callbacks for any signature, no trampoline pool, struct by value later). Fallback: a hand-written caller for scalar classes and a static trampoline pool.\n- Platforms: macOS arm64 and x86_64, Linux x86_64 and aarch64, Windows x64.\n- Errors: library not found, symbol missing, wrong argument count or type are exceptions at bind time or call time with the library, function and argument. A native that returns an error code is the script's business (a `check(rc)` helper in the extension); `@:throws` style sugar can come later.\n\nWorker lane:\n- Functions marked `@:blocking` (already captured in the flags) run on a worker thread, one lane per library so calls into one library never overlap; the calling fiber is Pending and resumes with the result.\n- A callback invoked on the worker is marshalled to the VM thread as a nested call; the worker waits for its result. A callback invoked on the VM thread runs directly. An exception in a callback is caught, the declared failure value is returned to C, and the exception is thrown after the call returns. Nothing unwinds through a C frame.\n- The host loop must wake for worker results and callback requests, not only for stdin.\n\nTrust and loading:\n- A library is loaded only if the extension's config declares it (`\"natives\": {\"git2\": {\"path\": \"natives/libgit2.dylib\", \"sha256\": \"...\"}}`); the host checks the hash. RayDE shows path and hash on the Extensions page. A built-in is pre-approved.\n- The host stays a separate process: a native fault takes that extension down, not the editor.\n\n\n- A fixture cdylib in `rayscript-vm/tests` (Rust, `extern \"C\"`) with functions of every width, doubles, strings in and out, out-params, nested structs and arrays, callbacks with user data (also stored in a struct), error returns, a function that sleeps (blocking lane), a function that calls back from another thread.\n- Overhead: a scalar call (`add`) benchmarked; target under 1 microsecond over a Rust closure native.\n- Integration: libgit2 opens a repository, lists status through `git_status_foreach`, and walks a diff with the four `git_diff_foreach` callbacks, from a script.","files":null}]}